.TH CLRU_SET 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clru_set \- store a value by key (type-safe convenience wrapper)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <clrucache.h>

ccol_retval_t clru_set(name, key, val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-safe GNU C statement expression that converts key and val to the
cache's own declared KeyT/ValT (the same way a plain C assignment would),
serialises the converted values into cmap_pair values, and calls
clrucache_set_full().

Converting val to ValT (rather than storing val's own natural expression
type verbatim) means an int literal passed for a double-valued cache
stores the double value 5.0, not a 4-byte copy of the int's own bit
pattern; a val (or key) expression whose type cannot be implicitly
converted to the cache's declared ValT (or KeyT) is a compile error at
this point.

If a remote setter was configured at construction it is called
synchronously before the cache is updated. On setter failure the
function returns ccol_unexpected_failure and the cache is left unchanged.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Cache variable. Must be a plain variable declared with
      clru_construct/clru_declare, or restored with clru_redeclare()
      in the current scope (this is what supplies KeyT/ValT for the
      conversion above); it cannot be an arbitrary expression such as
      a struct member access.
key   Key expression; any expression implicitly convertible to KeyT.
val   Value expression; any expression implicitly convertible to ValT.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success             Value stored (remote setter succeeded, if any).
ccol_unexpected_failure  Remote setter returned false; cache unchanged.
ccol_invalid_args        cache is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID or a stale
                         (already-destroyed) handle; or a null
                         pointer or zero-size argument.
ccol_not_enough_memory   Memory allocation for the new value failed.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
clru_construct(cache, int, double, 128, NULL, NULL, NULL);

int k = 42;
double v = 3.14;
clru_set(cache, k, v);

clru_destroy(cache);
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Uses a GNU C statement expression ({...}); requires GCC or Clang.
When passing the cache handle across scopes (e.g. into a thread
function), declare a local alias and call clru_redeclare() on it before
calling clru_set(), the same as clru_get() requires; see clru_redeclare(3).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_get (3),
.BR clrucache_set_full (3)
